 "caveats": "Only stocks still listed today (companies that were delisted or acquired are missing, which flatters all returns a little, but affects every bucket). Averages are trimmed at the 0.5% tails. Past results are evidence, not a promise.",
 "findings": [
  "In US stocks a longer base did NOT lead to a bigger move, the opposite of the Indian result. Among breakouts in an uptrend (above a rising 150-day and the 200-day average) with RS 70+, 50–99-day bases beat the S&P 500 by about 4% over the next year and 25–49-day bases by about 2%, while 200–399-day bases lagged by about 1%, 400–799-day bases by about 3% and 800+ day bases by about 6%. The order held in both 2007–16 and 2017–26.",
  "Long bases were steadier rather than stronger: fewer of their breakouts failed (about 25–31% for 400+ days against 37% for 25–49 days), but far fewer doubled within the year (2–4% against 8%).",
  "The US Base Score (0–100) was rebuilt from this data: RS rating 30, trend strength 35 (distance above the 200-day average, and a 150-day average rising 2–5%+ a month), base length 15 (50–99 days best), breakout-day volume 15 (3× the 50-day average best) and a base built after an advance 5. It was kept because it sorted returns in the same order in BOTH halves: 75+ beat the S&P 500 by 8.4% a year in 2007–16 and 12.2% in 2017–26, against +1.8% and −2.6% for under 30; the chance of doubling rose from 3% to 14% and from 7% to 25%.",
  "What the Base Score does NOT do: it does not lift the typical breakout. The median 75+ breakout still lagged the index (−0.7% and −5.0%), and high scores failed more often (39–49% closed back inside the base within 20 sessions, against 32–36% for under 30). The edge is in the winners, so cut failed breakouts quickly. A 60+ score beat a sub-30 score in 14 of 19 years; it lost in 2008, 2011, 2015 and 2021 (momentum sell-offs) and tied in 2016.",
  "RS mattered most in the last decade: in 2017–26 RS 95+ breakouts beat the S&P 500 by 14.3% a year and doubled 24% of the time, against −1.7% and 8% for RS under 50. In 2007–16 RS bands were close together on average (+2% to +3%), though RS 95+ still doubled most often (11.5% vs 2–4%).",
  "Tightness surprised: a right side within 6% over the last 15 sessions failed least (24–28%) but did worst (+0.8% and −5.8% a year), while a 16%+ right side did best (+8.4% and +13.9%) and doubled most. Tight bases are common in slow, low-volatility stocks. Tightness is therefore shown on the site but left out of the score; on a fast-moving leader it is still a sensible place for a low-risk entry.",
  "Context: from 2017 to 2026 a few giant companies carried the cap-weighted S&P 500, so the median stock lagged it and most buckets have negative medians. Compare the buckets with each other, not with zero. Only stocks listed today are included (survivorship), which flatters every bucket a little."
 ]
